            "A pressure injury is localised damage to the skin and underlying tissue, usually over a bony prominence, caused by <strong>pressure</strong> or pressure combined with <strong>shear</strong>. Sustained pressure squeezes capillaries shut; tissue starved of oxygen dies from the inside out — the muscle over the bone goes first, which is why a small purple patch on the sacrum can hide a cavity. The damage begins within <strong>one to two hours</strong> of unrelieved pressure, faster on a hard trolley, a spinal board or a theatre table, in the dehydrated or shocked patient, and in skin that is already wet. <strong>Shear</strong> — the skeleton sliding one way while the skin is held by the sheet, as a patient slips down a bed whose head is raised — tears and kinks the deep vessels. <strong>Friction</strong> from dragging a patient across the sheet strips the epidermis. <strong>Moisture</strong> from urine, stool, sweat and exudate softens the skin so that all three do more harm. The sites are where bone is close to the surface: the <strong>sacrum and buttocks</strong> (lying and sitting), the <strong>heels</strong>, the hips (lateral position), the elbows, shoulder blades, spine, back of the head, the ears and nose under tubing and masks, and under every plaster, splint, collar, catheter and oxygen tubing.",
